<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=submarine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Submarine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A submarine is a watercraft capable of independent operation underwater; the first submersible of which reliable information exists, propelled by oars, was built in 1620 by Cornelis Drebbel, a Dutchman in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a>. The first verified submarine able to operate and move independently underwater was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Bushnell's one-man Turtle of 1775, also the first military submersible. Navies could put submarines into routine service only once suitable engines were developed, in the late 19th century, and they were first used widely in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/submarine.jpg" alt="Submarine" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by General Dynamics Electric Boat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Navy_040730-N-1234E-002_PCU_Virginia_(SSN_774)_returns_to_the_General_Dynamics_Electric_Boat_shipyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1620</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Cornelis Drebbel · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Drebbel’s oar-driven boat was a submersible; David Bushnell’s Turtle of 1775 was the first verified submarine able to move underwater on its own.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=submarine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
